<section id="overview">
<div class="wrap">
  <div class="section-head">
    <div class="eyebrow">01 · The opportunity</div>
    <h2>Starting a new job shouldn't feel like finding your way through a maze.</h2>
    <p>
      A new employee often has to absorb a lot of information in a short
      period of time. FirstSteps explores how onboarding can feel more
      organized, approachable, and easier to navigate.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="copy">
      <p>
        Starting a new role involves more than learning job responsibilities.
        Employees may need to understand company policies, meet colleagues,
        complete required training, learn unfamiliar systems, and figure out
        where to find help.
      </p>

      <p>
        When information is spread across emails, documents, meetings, and
        different platforms, it can be difficult to know what matters most
        or what to do next.
      </p>

      <p>
        FirstSteps is a proposed onboarding experience that brings key
        activities and resources into one place. It gives new employees a
        clearer path while allowing them to revisit information when needed.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="highlight">
      <strong>The design opportunity</strong>
      <p>
        Create a guided onboarding experience that balances structured
        learning with flexibility, helping employees understand their
        priorities without overwhelming them with information.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
</section>

<section>
<div class="wrap">
  <div class="section-head">
    <div class="eyebrow">02 · Learner needs</div>
    <h2>What does a new employee need during onboarding?</h2>
    <p>
      The experience is designed around common onboarding needs that
      organizations can validate with new employees and managers.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="audience-card">
      <h3>Primary audience</h3>
      <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:13px;">
        Employees who are new to an organization and need support
        understanding their role, workplace expectations, and essential
        processes.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>New hires joining an unfamiliar team</li>
        <li>Employees learning new systems and processes</li>
        <li>People completing required introductory training</li>
        <li>Employees who need to revisit information independently</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="audience-card">
      <h3>Key learner needs</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Understand what to complete and when</li>
        <li>Know where to find policies and essential resources</li>
        <li>Understand who to contact for support</li>
        <li>Track onboarding activities and progress</li>
        <li>Connect learning activities to actual job responsibilities</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
</section>

<section>
<div class="wrap">
  <div class="section-head">
    <div class="eyebrow">03 · The challenge</div>
    <h2>Three onboarding challenges to address.</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="challenge-grid">
    <article class="challenge">
      <div class="number">01</div>
      <h3>Information overload</h3>
      <p>
        New employees receive a large amount of information before they
        have enough context to understand or remember it.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="challenge">
      <div class="number">02</div>
      <h3>Unclear next steps</h3>
      <p>
        Employees may not know which activities are essential, what
        deadlines matter, or what to prioritize first.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="challenge">
      <div class="number">03</div>
      <h3>Scattered resources</h3>
      <p>
        Important documents, training materials, and contacts can be
        difficult to locate when they are spread across platforms.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="journey">
<div class="wrap">
  <div class="section-head">
    <div class="eyebrow">04 · Proposed learning journey</div>
    <h2>A guided first 30 days.</h2>
    <p>
      Rather than presenting everything at once, FirstSteps organizes
      onboarding into manageable stages. The timing and activities would
      be adapted to the role and organization.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="journey-grid">
    <article class="phase">
      <div class="phase-label">Days 1–5</div>
      <h3>Get oriented</h3>
      <p>Help employees understand the organization and get comfortable.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Welcome and orientation</li>
        <li>Meet the team and manager</li>
        <li>Set up essential accounts</li>
        <li>Review workplace policies</li>
        <li>Explore key resources</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="phase">
      <div class="phase-label">Days 6–15</div>
      <h3>Build understanding</h3>
      <p>Introduce important responsibilities, tools, and processes.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Complete role-specific learning</li>
        <li>Explore systems and tools</li>
        <li>Review process documentation</li>
        <li>Observe team workflows</li>
        <li>Ask questions and get feedback</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="phase">
      <div class="phase-label">Days 16–30</div>
      <h3>Put learning into practice</h3>
      <p>Support employees as they begin applying what they have learned.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Practise common job tasks</li>
        <li>Complete guided activities</li>
        <li>Revisit reference materials</li>
        <li>Meet with a manager or mentor</li>
        <li>Reflect on progress and next steps</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
</div>
</section>

